# =============================================================================
# Copy to `.env.local` and fill in. Never commit real values.
#
# NOTE: there are deliberately NO SPOTIFY CREDENTIALS in this file, and none are
# needed. Phase 0 established that Spotify's Feb 2026 Web API changes make the
# official API unusable for "anyone with a public link" (Client Credentials can no
# longer read playlist items, and Development Mode apps cap at 5 invited users), so
# the app reads the public embed endpoint with no login at all. If you find
# yourself adding a client ID or secret here, re-read `docs/plans/plan.md` §2 first.
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Year cache and rate-limit gates (Phase 2; widened 2026-09-30)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Upstash Redis REST endpoint. Backs FIVE things, not one:
#   1. the MusicBrainz year cache (`mbyear:`), shared across all users so repeat
#      playlists resolve instantly
#   2. the per-provider answer cache (`yearprov:deezer:`, `yearprov:itunes:`), which
#      is how `/api/year?stage=verify` reads what `stage=resolve` already found --
#      from Redis, never from the client
#   3-5. THREE rate-limit gates, one per year provider: MusicBrainz 1.1 s, Deezer
#      120 ms, iTunes 3 s. Each only enforces the real global limit when it is backed
#      by a shared lock, because every request leaves from Vercel's shared egress IPs
#      -- see api/_lib/rate-limit.ts
# Required in PRODUCTION only. Without it all of them fall back to per-instance
# versions and everything still works; the fallback is logged once at cold start so it
# is never silent. Setting this WITHOUT the token below logs a warning and stays
# in-memory. Two consequences worth knowing under `vercel dev`, which runs a fresh
# process per request: `verify` finds none of `resolve`'s answers and asks every
# provider again, and iTunes is sent UNPACED traffic from your own IP -- it answers
# excess with 403s, which leave iTunes out of the vote (a refusal is a skip). Configure
# Upstash before resolving a whole deck locally.
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL=

# Auth token for the Upstash Redis REST endpoint above.
# Required in PRODUCTION only.
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MusicBrainz (Phase 2)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MusicBrainz REQUIRES a descriptive User-Agent identifying the application and a
# contact address, and rate-limits to 1 request/second. Requests without this are
# liable to be blocked. This is also the reason year lookups must go through a
# serverless function: browsers cannot set the User-Agent header.
# Format: AppName/Version ( contact-email-or-url )
#
# SKIPPED WITH A WARNING when unset, not required (2026-09-30). The staged
# `/api/year?stage=resolve|verify` asks three providers, and every one of them is
# skippable: without this variable MusicBrainz is left out of the vote, the response
# lists it in `skipped`, and a `[year-pipeline] musicbrainz skipped` warning is logged
# once per cold start (naming the variable, never its value). The deck still gets
# years from Deezer and iTunes, with less coverage of old catalogue.
#
# The STAGE-LESS legacy `/api/year` (tabs still on the old client) has only
# MusicBrainz, so there an unset variable is still the loud 500 `not-configured` on
# EVERY request, including ones that would have hit the cache.
#
# Set it in LOCAL DEVELOPMENT and PRODUCTION all the same. Put a real contact address
# in it: it is sent to a third party on every lookup.
MUSICBRAINZ_USER_AGENT=custom-jitster/0.1.0 ( you@example.com )

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deezer and iTunes (2026-09-30)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Nothing to set. Both are keyless public search APIs, so there is deliberately NO
# variable for either -- and none for Discogs, which was measured and dropped from the
# provider plan (docs/spikes/spike.year-fetch-rework.md §13.10-13.12). The iTunes
# storefront is a constant in api/_lib/itunes.ts, not a variable.
